ties to each product's count\n\nFor `getConversionRate`:\n- Track unique cartIds from `cart:updated`\n- Track order count from `order:created`\n- Return orders / unique carts\n\nFor `getRevenue`:\n- Listen to `payment:processed` → if status is SUCCESS, add amount to revenue\n\nWait, `payment:processed` has `{ orderId, status, transactionId?, error? }` but no amount! So how does analytics know the revenue?\n\nHmm, I need to track the order total from `order:created` events and then correlate with payment success. When a payment is processed successfully for an order, I add that order's total to revenue.\n\nOK, let me implement that.\n\nLet me write all the code now. I'll be careful with types and make sure everything compiles.\n\nOne more thing - the `emit` method is async, so I need to handle that. In most cases, I'll just fire and forget (not await) since the services don't need to wait for event handlers to complete. But I should be careful about the order of operations.\n\nActually, looking at the interface signatures, none of the service methods (except PaymentService.processPayment) return Promises. So I'll fire-and-forget the emit calls in the synchronous methods.\n\nWait, but `emit` returns `Promise<void>`. If I don't await it, any errors will be silently swallowed. But since the service interfaces are synchronous, I have no choice but to not await them.\n\nActually, I should be careful. Let me look at the interfaces again:\n- Catalog: all synchronous\n- Inventory: all synchronous\n- Cart: all synchronous\n- Pricing: `calculateTotal` is synchronous\n- Orders: all synchronous\n- Payment: `processPayment` is async, `refund` is async\n- Notifications: all synchronous\n- Analytics: all synchronous\n\nSo for the synchronous methods, I'll fire-and-forget the emits. For the async payment methods, I can await the emits.\n\nLet me now write the code.\n\nFor the payment service options, I need to figure out the shape. The spec says \"configurable simulation of success, failure, and timeout scenarios via an options parameter\". I'll define:\n```typescript\ninterface PaymentOptions {\n  simulateSuccess?: boolean;\n  simulateFailure?: boolean;\n  simulateTimeout?: number; // ms to wait before timing out\n}\n```\n\nDefault behavior (no options or no simulation flags): simulate success.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider. If `simulateFailure` is true, it should fail. If `simulateSuccess` is true (or default), it should succeed. If `simulateTimeout` is set, it should wait that long and then return timeout.\n\nI think the priority should be: if simulateFailure, fail; if simulateTimeout, timeout; otherwise succeed.\n\nOr maybe: if simulateFailure is true, always fail; if simulateTimeout > 0, wait then timeout; otherwise succeed.\n\nLet me just make it simple:\n- If `simulateFailure` is true → return FAILED\n- If `simulateTimeout` is a positive number → wait that many ms, then return TIMEOUT\n- Otherwise (default) → return SUCCESS\n\nNow let me implement all services.\n\nLet me also consider: should I use `crypto.randomUUID()` instead of the `uuid` package? Both work. Since `uuid` is a dependency, I'll use it. Actually, `crypto.randomUUID()` is simpler and doesn't require an import. Let me use that instead to keep things simple.\n\nActually wait, with the strict TypeScript config and the Node types, `crypto.randomUUID()` should be available. But I need to make sure I import it correctly. In Node.js ESM, I'd do `import { randomUUID } from 'crypto'`. That's cleaner than using the uuid package.\n\nLet me use `crypto.randomUUID()` for ID generation.\n\nNow, let me write all the service implementations.\n\nLet me draft each one:\n\n### Catalog Service\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { CatalogService, Events, ProductCreatedEvent } from '../types.js';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'crypto';\n\nexport function createCatalogService(bus: EventBus): CatalogService {\n  const products = new Map<string, { id: string; name: string; price: number; category: string }>();\n\n  return {\n    createProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string {\n      const id = randomUUID();\n      products.set(id, { id, name, price, category });\n      bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, { productId: id, name, price, category } as ProductCreatedEvent);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getProduct(productId: string) {\n      return products.get(productId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    listProducts(category?: string) {\n      const all = Array.from(products.values());\n      if (category) {\n        return all.filter(p => p.category === category);\n      }\n      return all;\n    },\n\n    searchProducts(query: string) {\n      const q = query.toLowerCase();\n      return Array.from(products.values()).filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(q));\n    },\n\n    updateProduct(productId: string, updates: Partial<{ name: string; price: number; category: string }>): void {\n      const product = products.get(productId);\n      if (product) {\n        if (updates.name !== undefined) product.name = updates.name;\n        if (updates.price !== undefined) product.price = updates.price;\n        if (updates.category !== undefined) product.category = updates.category;\n      }\n    },\n\n    removeProduct(productId: string): boolean {\n      return products.delete(productId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Inventory Service\n\nFor reserve/release, I need to track both available stock and reserved quantities. The `quantity` in `setStock` is the total available. When reserving, we deduct from available. When releasing, we add back.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The `getStock` method returns the current quantity. After reserving 3 from a stock of 10, what should `getStock` return? I think it should return the available (unreserved) quantity, which would be 7.\n\nWait, actually, in typical inventory systems, \"stock\" could mean either total or available. Let me look at the event: `InventoryUpdatedEvent` has `{ productId, quantity }`. And `LowStockAlertEvent` has `{ productId, currentQuantity, threshold }`.\n\nI think `quantity` in the event is the available quantity. And `getStock` returns the available quantity. Reserved is tracked internally but not exposed.\n\nSo:\n- `setStock(productId, 10)` → available = 10, reserved = 0\n- `reserve(productId, 3)` → available = 7, reserved = 3\n- `release(productId, 2)` → available = 9, reserved = 1\n- `getStock(productId)` → returns 9 (available)\n\nFor low stock alerts, we check if the available quantity falls below the threshold after a `setStock`, `reserve`, or `release` operation.\n\nActually, should we check on release too? Releasing increases stock, so it would go above the threshold, not below. Unless the threshold is very high. Let me just check after any stock change.\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { InventoryService, Events, InventoryUpdatedEvent, LowStockAlertEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createInventoryService(bus: EventBus): InventoryService {\n  const stock = new Map<string, number>(); // available quantity\n  const thresholds = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  function checkLowStock(productId: string): void {\n    const quantity = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    const threshold = thresholds.get(productId);\n    if (threshold !== undefined && quantity <= threshold) {\n      // Wait, should it be < or <=? \"falls below configurable thresholds\"\n      // \"falls below\" typically means < threshold, but \"at or below\" would be <=\n      // Let me use < for \"falls below\"\n      if (quantity < threshold) {\n        bus.emit(Events.LOW_STOCK_ALERT, { productId, currentQuantity: quantity, threshold } as LowStockAlertEvent);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    setStock(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      stock.set(productId, quantity);\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, { productId, quantity } as InventoryUpdatedEvent);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n\n    getStock(productId: string): number {\n      return stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    },\n\n    reserve(productId: string, quantity: number): boolean {\n      const current = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      if (current < quantity) return false;\n      stock.set(productId, current - quantity);\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, { productId, quantity: stock.get(productId)! } as InventoryUpdatedEvent);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n      return true;\n    },\n\n    release(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const current = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      stock.set(productId, current + quantity);\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, { productId, quantity: stock.get(productId)! } as InventoryUpdatedEvent);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n\n    setLowStockThreshold(productId: string, threshold: number): void {\n      thresholds.set(productId, threshold);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, \"falls below\" - I'll use `<` for \"below\". But actually, \"low stock\" typically means \"at or below the threshold\". Let me use `<=` since that's more conventional for inventory alerts. The spec says \"Detect low-stock conditions and alert when stock falls below configurable thresholds.\" Hmm, \"falls below\" literally means going under, so `<`. But practically, when stock equals the threshold, you'd want an alert. Let me use `<=`.\n\nActually, I just realized - let me be more careful. The typical interpretation in e-commerce is that the threshold IS the low stock level, so if stock is AT the threshold, it's already low. Let me use `<=`.\n\nWait, but what if I set threshold to 5 and stock to 5? Is that \"low stock\"? In most systems, yes. Let me use `<=`.\n\n### Cart Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { CartService, Events, CartUpdatedEvent } from '../types.js';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'crypto';\n\nexport function createCartService(bus: EventBus): CartService {\n  const carts = new Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; items: Map<string, number> }>();\n\n  function toItemsArray(items: Map<string, number>): Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number }> {\n    return Array.from(items.entries()).map(([productId, quantity]) => ({ productId, quantity }));\n  }\n\n  function emitUpdated(cart: { id: string; userId: string; items: Map<string, number> }): void {\n    bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, {\n      cartId: cart.id,\n      userId: cart.userId,\n      items: toItemsArray(cart.items),\n    } as CartUpdatedEvent);\n  }\n\n  return {\n    createCart(userId: string): string {\n      const id = randomUUID();\n      const cart = { id, userId, items: new Map<string, number>() };\n      carts.set(id, cart);\n      emitUpdated(cart);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    addItem(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      const current = cart.items.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      cart.items.set(productId, current + quantity);\n      emitUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    removeItem(cartId: string, productId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items.delete(productId);\n      emitUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    getCart(cartId: string) {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return null;\n      return { id: cart.id, userId: cart.userId, items: toItemsArray(cart.items) };\n    },\n\n    clearCart(cartId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items.clear();\n      emitUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    updateQuantity(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      if (quantity <= 0) {\n        cart.items.delete(productId);\n      } else {\n        cart.items.set(productId, quantity);\n      }\n      emitUpdated(cart);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Pricing Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { PricingService, Events, ProductCreatedEvent, PriceCalculatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createPricingService(bus: EventBus): PricingService {\n  const prices = new Map<string, number>();\n  const discountCodes = new Map<string, number>(); // code -> percentage\n\n  bus.on(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as ProductCreatedEvent;\n    prices.set(event.productId, event.price);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    calculateTotal(items: Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number; price: number }>, discountCode?: string) {\n      const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);\n      let discount = 0;\n      if (discountCode && discountCodes.has(discountCode)) {\n        const percentage = discountCodes.get(discountCode)!;\n        discount = subtotal * (percentage / 100);\n      }\n      const taxableAmount = subtotal - discount;\n      const tax = taxableAmount * 0.10;\n      const total = taxableAmount + tax;\n      \n      const result = { subtotal, discount, tax, total };\n      bus.emit(Events.PRICE_CALCULATED, { ...result, cartId: '' } as PriceCalculatedEvent);\n      // Hmm, the PriceCalculatedEvent has a cartId field. But calculateTotal doesn't receive a cartId.\n      // Let me just emit without cartId or use an empty string.\n      // Actually, looking at the event definition: `PriceCalculatedEvent { cartId: string; subtotal: number; discount: number; tax: number; total: number; }`\n      // The calculateTotal method doesn't have a cartId parameter. I'll emit without one or with empty string.\n      // Actually, maybe I shouldn't emit the event at all if there's no cartId? Or maybe the event should just be emitted with what we have.\n      // Let me just emit with the data we have. The cartId can be empty string since it's not available in this context.\n      \n      return result;\n    },\n\n    addDiscountCode(code: string, percentage: number): void {\n      discountCodes.set(code, percentage);\n    },\n\n    getPrice(productId: string): number {\n      return prices.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the PriceCalculatedEvent has a cartId but calculateTotal doesn't take one. Let me just not emit the event, or emit it with an empty cartId. I think emitting it is important for analytics. Let me emit with an empty string or omit the emit entirely.\n\nActually, looking at the requirements again: \"Calculate order totals including tax (10% rate). Support registering percentage-based discount codes that reduce the subtotal before tax is applied. Listen for product creation events to track product prices.\" It doesn't explicitly say to emit events. But the event type exists. Let me just not emit from calculateTotal since there's no cartId available, and the spec doesn't explicitly require it.\n\nWait, actually I should be consistent. Let me look at what events each service should emit based on the Events constants and the spec:\n\n- Catalog: emits `PRODUCT_CREATED` ✓\n- Inventory: emits `INVENTORY_UPDATED`, `LOW_STOCK_ALERT` ✓\n- Cart: emits `CART_UPDATED` ✓\n- Pricing: emits `PRICE_CALCULATED` (implied by event existence, but no cartId in method signature)\n- Orders: emits `ORDER_CREATED`, `ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED` ✓\n- Payment: emits `PAYMENT_PROCESSED` ✓\n- Notifications: emits `NOTIFICATION_SENT` ✓\n- Analytics: emits `ANALYTICS_EVENT` (maybe?)\n\nFor Pricing, I'll emit PRICE_CALCULATED with an empty cartId since the interface doesn't provide one. Actually, let me just skip the emit for now. The event exists but the service interface doesn't support it cleanly. If tests require it, I can add it.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. Maybe I should emit the event. The tests might check for it. Let me emit with the cartId as empty string.\n\nHmm, actually let me not emit it. The PriceCalculatedEvent has a required cartId and there's no way to provide it from the calculateTotal method. Emitting with an empty string would be wrong. If tests need it, they'll fail and I can fix it.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just emit it. The event might be important for analytics. I'll use an empty string for cartId.\n\nWait, I just realized - the spec doesn't say Pricing should emit events. It says \"Listen for product creation events to track product prices.\" So the only event interaction is listening. Let me not emit PRICE_CALCULATED.\n\nOK, let me not emit from Pricing. If needed, I can add it later.\n\n### Orders Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { OrderService, Events, OrderCreatedEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent, OrderStatus } from '../types.js';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'crypto';\n\nexport function createOrderService(bus: EventBus): OrderService {\n  const orders = new Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number }>();\n\n  return {\n    createOrder(userId: string, cartId: string, total: number): string {\n      const id = randomUUID();\n      orders.set(id, { id, userId, status: OrderStatus.PENDING, total });\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, { orderId: id, userId, cartId, total } as OrderCreatedEvent);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getOrder(orderId: string) {\n      return orders.get(orderId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    updateStatus(orderId: string, status: OrderStatus): void {\n      const order = orders.get(orderId);\n      if (!order) return;\n      const previousStatus = order.status;\n      order.status = status;\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, { orderId, status, previousStatus } as OrderStatusChangedEvent);\n    },\n\n    listOrders(userId: string) {\n      return Array.from(orders.values())\n        .filter(o => o.userId === userId)\n        .map(o => ({ id: o.id, status: o.status, total: o.total }));\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Payment Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { PaymentService, Events, PaymentProcessedEvent, PaymentStatus } from '../types.js';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'crypto';\n\nexport interface PaymentOptions {\n  simulateSuccess?: boolean;\n  simulateFailure?: boolean;\n  simulateTimeout?: number;\n}\n\nexport function createPaymentService(bus: EventBus, options?: PaymentOptions): PaymentService {\n  const transactions = new Map<string, { orderId: string; amount: number; status: PaymentStatus }>();\n\n  return {\n    async processPayment(orderId: string, amount: number) {\n      if (options?.simulateFailure) {\n        const result = { status: PaymentStatus.FAILED, error: 'Payment failed' };\n        await bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, { orderId, ...result } as PaymentProcessedEvent);\n        return result;\n      }\n\n      if (options?.simulateTimeout && options.simulateTimeout > 0) {\n        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, options.simulateTimeout));\n        const result = { status: PaymentStatus.TIMEOUT, error: 'Payment timed out' };\n        await bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, { orderId, ...result } as PaymentProcessedEvent);\n        return result;\n      }\n\n      // Default: success\n      const transactionId = randomUUID();\n      transactions.set(transactionId, { orderId, amount, status: PaymentStatus.SUCCESS });\n      const result = { status: PaymentStatus.SUCCESS, transactionId };\n      await bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, { orderId, ...result } as PaymentProcessedEvent);\n      return result;\n    },\n\n    async refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean> {\n      const transaction = transactions.get(transactionId);\n      if (!transaction || transaction.status !== PaymentStatus.SUCCESS) {\n        return false;\n      }\n      transaction.status = PaymentStatus.FAILED; // Mark as refunded\n      // Hmm, there's no REFUNDED status. Let me just remove it or mark it differently.\n      // Actually, I should just track that it's been refunded. Let me add a refunded flag.\n      // But the interface only has status. Let me just return true and not change the status.\n      // Actually, the refund should mark it somehow. But PaymentStatus doesn't have a REFUNDED status.\n      // Let me just return true and keep the transaction as is. The refund just needs to return true.\n      return true;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, for the refund, I need to make sure a transaction can't be refunded twice. Let me add a `refunded` flag.\n\nAlso, I need to store the transactionId mapping so that refund can look it up. Let me revise:\n\n```typescript\nconst transactions = new Map<string, { orderId: string; amount: number; status: PaymentStatus; refunded?: boolean }>();\n\nasync refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean> {\n  const transaction = transactions.get(transactionId);\n  if (!transaction || transaction.status !== PaymentStatus.SUCCESS || transaction.refunded) {\n    return false;\n  }\n  transaction.refunded = true;\n  return true;\n}\n```\n\n### Notifications Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { NotificationService, Events, NotificationSentEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent, PaymentProcessedEvent, OrderCreatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createNotificationService(bus: EventBus): NotificationService {\n  const subscribers = new Set<string>();\n  const notifications = new Map<string, Array<{ type: string; message: string; timestamp: number }>>();\n  const orderUsers = new Map<string, string>(); // orderId -> userId\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderUsers.set(event.orderId, event.userId);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderStatusChangedEvent;\n    const userId = orderUsers.get(event.orderId);\n    if (userId && subscribers.has(userId)) {\n      const notification = {\n        type: 'order_status_changed',\n        message: `Order ${event.orderId} status changed to ${event.status}`,\n        timestamp: Date.now(),\n      };\n      if (!notifications.has(userId)) {\n        notifications.set(userId, []);\n      }\n      notifications.get(userId)!.push(notification);\n      bus.emit(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, { userId, ...notification } as NotificationSentEvent);\n    }\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    const userId = orderUsers.get(event.orderId);\n    if (userId && subscribers.has(userId)) {\n      const notification = {\n        type: 'payment_processed',\n        message: `Payment for order ${event.orderId} ${event.status}`,\n        timestamp: Date.now(),\n      };\n      if (!notifications.has(userId)) {\n        notifications.set(userId, []);\n      }\n      notifications.get(userId)!.push(notification);\n      bus.emit(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, { userId, ...notification } as NotificationSentEvent);\n    }\n  });\n\n  return {\n    subscribe(userId: string): void {\n      subscribers.add(userId);\n    },\n\n    getNotifications(userId: string) {\n      return notifications.get(userId) ?? [];\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Analytics Service\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { AnalyticsService, Events, OrderCreatedEvent, PaymentProcessedEvent, CartUpdatedEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent, ProductCreatedEvent, InventoryUpdatedEvent, LowStockAlertEvent, PriceCalculatedEvent, NotificationSentEvent, PaymentStatus } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createAnalyticsService(bus: EventBus): AnalyticsService {\n  const eventCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let revenue = 0;\n  const uniqueCarts = new Set<string>();\n  let orderCount = 0;\n  const productOrderCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  const cartItems = new Map<string, Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number }>>();\n  const orderTotals = new Map<string, number>(); // orderId -> total\n\n  function incrementEvent(eventType: string): void {\n    eventCounts.set(eventType, (eventCounts.get(eventType) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n\n  // Subscribe to all events\n  bus.on(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.LOW_STOCK_ALERT, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.LOW_STOCK_ALERT);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.CART_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.CART_UPDATED);\n    const event = payload as CartUpdatedEvent;\n    uniqueCarts.add(event.cartId);\n    cartItems.set(event.cartId, event.items);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.PRICE_CALCULATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.PRICE_CALCULATED);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.ORDER_CREATED);\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderCount++;\n    orderTotals.set(event.orderId, event.total);\n    uniqueCarts.add(event.cartId);\n    // Track product quantities from the cart\n    const items = cartItems.get(event.cartId);\n    if (items) {\n      for (const item of items) {\n        productOrderCounts.set(item.productId, (productOrderCounts.get(item.productId) ?? 0) + item.quantity);\n      }\n    }\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED);\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    if (event.status === PaymentStatus.SUCCESS) {\n      const total = orderTotals.get(event.orderId) ?? 0;\n      revenue += total;\n    }\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, (payload: unknown) => {\n    incrementEvent(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    getEventCount(eventType: string): number {\n      return eventCounts.get(eventType) ?? 0;\n    },\n\n    getRevenue(): number {\n      return revenue;\n    },\n\n    getConversionRate(): number {\n      if (uniqueCarts.size === 0) return 0;\n      return orderCount / uniqueCarts.size;\n    },\n\n    getTopProducts(limit: number): Array<{ productId: string; count: number }> {\n      return Array.from(productOrderCounts.entries())\n        .map(([productId, count]) => ({ productId, count }))\n        .sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count)\n        .slice(0, limit);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, for getConversionRate, the spec says \"orders divided by unique carts\". I'm tracking unique carts from `cart:updated` events AND from `order:created` events (via cartId). But the conversion rate should be orders / unique carts. A \"cart\" is created when `createCart` is called, which emits `cart:updated`. An order is created when `createOrder` is called. So:\n\n- uniqueCarts should count all carts that were ever created (from cart:updated events)\n- orderCount should count all orders created\n\nThe conversion rate = orderCount / uniqueCarts.size\n\nBut I'm also adding the cartId from order:created to uniqueCarts, which is redundant since it should already be there from the cart:updated event. But it's harmless - sets deduplicate.\n\nActually, wait. What if an order is created but the cart:updated event wasn't processed yet? Since emit is async and the handlers are called in order, this shouldn't happen in practice if the cart was created before the order. But to be safe, I'll keep both sources.\n\nHmm, actually, there's a subtle issue. The `cart:updated` event is emitted every time the cart changes, not just when it's created. So uniqueCarts will correctly track all cart IDs from the first `cart:updated` event for each cart.\n\nOK, I think my implementation is correct. Let me also consider: should the analytics service emit `ANALYTICS_EVENT`? The spec says \"Track platform-wide metrics by listening to events on the