tics service emit this? The spec says \"Track platform-wide metrics by listening to events on the bus.\" It doesn't say to emit analytics events. But the event type exists. Maybe the analytics service should emit an ANALYTICS_EVENT for each event it processes?\n\nLet me not emit it for now. The spec focuses on listening and tracking, not emitting.\n\nAlso, I need to think about the `getConversionRate` method. It says \"orders divided by unique carts\". What counts as a \"unique cart\"? I think it's the number of distinct carts that were created. So I should track unique cartIds from CART_UPDATED events.\n\nBut wait, CART_UPDATED is emitted every time the cart changes (add item, remove item, clear, etc.). The first CART_UPDATED for a cart is when it's created. So tracking unique cartIds from CART_UPDATED gives me the total number of carts created. That's correct.\n\nActually, let me re-examine. When a cart is created, the CartService emits CART_UPDATED with the empty items list. When items are added, it emits again. So the set of unique cartIds from CART_UPDATED events is the set of all carts ever created. Good.\n\nFor the conversion rate: `orderCount / uniqueCarts.size`. If there are 10 carts and 3 orders, the conversion rate is 0.3.\n\nBut I'm also adding the cartId from ORDER_CREATED to uniqueCarts. This is redundant if the cart was already tracked from CART_UPDATED. But it handles the edge case where the order is created before the cart update event is processed (which shouldn't happen, but just in case).\n\nActually, there's a real issue here. The ORDER_CREATED event includes a `cartId`. If I add this to `uniqueCarts`, it could increase the denominator. But this cart should already be in `uniqueCarts` from the CART_UPDATED event. So it's harmless.\n\nUnless... the ORDER_CREATED event fires before the CART_UPDATED event for a new cart. But that doesn't make sense in the normal flow - you'd create a cart first, then create an order from it.\n\nOK, I think my implementation is correct. Let me finalize and write the files.\n\nActually, one more thing about the Pricing service. The `calculateTotal` method returns `{ subtotal, discount, tax, total }` but the `PriceCalculatedEvent` has `{ cartId, subtotal, discount, tax, total }`. The return type of `calculateTotal` doesn't include `cartId`, but the event does. So I can't emit the event without a cartId.\n\nLet me just not emit PRICE_CALCULATED. The spec for Pricing doesn't mention emitting events.\n\nOK, I'm confident in my design. Let me write all the files.\n\nActually, I want to double-check one thing. The `listOrders` method returns `Array<{ id: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number }>`. It doesn't include `userId` in the return type (even though the internal storage has it). This is correct - it's just listing orders for a given userId, so the userId is implicit.\n\nAlso, `getOrder` returns `{ id: string; userId: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number } | null`. This includes userId.\n\nOK, everything checks out. Let me write the code.\n\nOne more thing - for the Notifications service, I'm using `async` handlers because they emit events. But the EventBus's `emit` method awaits each handler. If a handler is `async` but doesn't actually do anything asynchronous (no `await`), it still returns a Promise that resolves immediately. So there's no issue.\n\nBut in my Notifications handlers, I'm awaiting the inner `bus.emit`. This means the Notifications handler won't return until the NOTIFICATION_SENT event is fully processed. This could cause issues if the NOTIFICATION_SENT handler itself emits events (which it doesn't in our services).\n\nWait, the Analytics service listens to NOTIFICATION_SENT. Its handler just increments a counter (synchronous). So the flow is:\n\n1. `bus.emit(ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, ...)` is called\n2. Notifications handler is called (async) → emits NOTIFICATION_SENT → Analytics handler for NOTIFICATION_SENT increments count → returns → Notifications handler returns\n3. Analytics handler for ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED increments count → returns\n4. emit returns\n\nThis is fine.\n\nBut wait, what's the order of handlers? The EventBus uses a `Set<EventHandler>`, which preserves insertion order in modern JavaScript. So handlers are called in the order they were registered. If Notifications registers its handlers before Analytics, then Notifications handlers run first. This depends on the order in which the factory functions are called.\n\nIn a typical setup, all services are created at startup. The order of creation determines the handler order. But the spec doesn't specify a particular order, so I shouldn't rely on it.\n\nThe important thing is that my implementation is correct regardless of handler order. The Analytics service tracks events independently, so the order of counter increments doesn't matter.\n\nOK, I'm ready to write the code. Let me create all files now.\n\nLet me also think about whether I should use `void` to explicitly ignore the Promise from `bus.emit` in sync methods. In TypeScript with strict mode, calling an async function without awaiting it is not an error, but it might generate a linting warning. Let me use `void bus.emit(...)` to be explicit.\n\nActually, looking at the ESLint config, it extends `eslint:recommended` and `plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended`. The `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` rule is not in the recommended set, so it won't be triggered. And `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` ignores variables starting with `_`.\n\nBut `void` is a good practice. Let me use it for sync methods that call `bus.emit`.\n\nWait, `void` as an expression is fine in TypeScript. `void bus.emit(...)` evaluates the emit and discards the result. But it might look odd to some readers. Let me just not use `void` and let the Promise float. The ESLint config doesn't flag it.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just not worry about it. The code will work fine either way.\n\nLet me now write all the files. I'll write them all at once.\n\nHmm, actually I should also think about whether the `PaymentOptions` type should be defined in `types.ts` or in `src/payment/index.ts`. Since `types.ts` is read-only and doesn't include it, I'll define it in `src/payment/index.ts`.\n\nAlso, I should export `PaymentOptions` from `src/index.ts` so consumers can use it.\n\nOK, let me write all the files now. Here's my final plan:\n\n1. `src/catalog/index.ts` - CatalogService factory\n2. `src/inventory/index.ts` - InventoryService factory\n3. `src/cart/index.ts` - CartService factory\n4. `src/pricing/index.ts` - PricingService factory\n5. `src/orders/index.ts` - OrderService factory\n6. `src/payment/index.ts` - PaymentService factory\n7. `src/notifications/index.ts` - NotificationService factory\n8. `src/analytics/index.ts` - AnalyticsService factory\n9. `src/index.ts` - Re-exports all factory functions\n\nLet me write them all now.","signature":"aa189df71d304af3a8c5459179b63104"}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.1-FP8","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":18772,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd81fee0-187e-4e4c-a1be-d6b34a22c525","uuid":"d3a2cd0c-4eef-4888-b0f6-3016d047dd0f"}
