led (sync), reads orderTotals, adds revenue\n13. etc.\n\nSo the order of microtasks depends on when they were scheduled. Microtask 1 was scheduled before Microtask 2, so it runs first. This means the ORDER_CREATED second handler runs before the PAYMENT_PROCESSED second handler. So the revenue should be tracked correctly!\n\nBut wait, the test checks `analytics.getRevenue()` after `await payment.processPayment(...)`. The `processPayment` function returns after step 5-9 (the bus.emit in processPayment is not awaited). So when the test checks getRevenue(), the microtasks from steps 10-13 haven't run yet!\n\nThe issue is that `processPayment` doesn't await its `bus.emit` call. So the PAYMENT_PROCESSED handlers might not have all executed by the time the test checks getRevenue().\n\nOK so the fix is clear: I need to `await bus.emit(...)` in `processPayment`. Let me check all services for unhandled bus.emit calls:\n\n- catalog: createProduct calls bus.emit (sync method, can't await)\n- inventory: setStock calls bus.emit (sync method, can't await)\n- cart: emits in multiple sync methods (can't await)\n- pricing: calculateTotal calls bus.emit (sync method, can't await)\n- orders: createOrder and updateStatus call bus.emit (sync methods, can't await)\n- payment: processPayment calls bus.emit (async method, CAN and SHOULD await)\n\nSo the only one I can fix is processPayment. Let me await bus.emit there.\n\nBut that still won't fix the integration test completely, because the ORDER_CREATED event from createOrder isn't awaited. However, as I analyzed above, the microtask ordering should ensure ORDER_CREATED handlers complete before PAYMENT_PROCESSED handlers.\n\nActually wait, if I await bus.emit in processPayment, then:\n1. createOrder calls bus.emit(ORDER_CREATED) - not awaited, first handler runs synchronously\n2. await payment.processPayment(orderId, total)\n3. Inside processPayment, await bus.emit(PAYMENT_PROCESSED)\n4. PAYMENT_PROCESSED first handler runs synchronously\n